Human-Critical Security Invariance for Advanced Multi-Agent Systems


Ouadi Maakoul and SI TEAM

Date: 15 October 2026

Abstract

The rapid development of increasingly capable AI agents creates a security problem that behavioral alignment, model-level safety training, and isolated execution environments cannot adequately address. An agent may generate unauthorized actions, exploit weaknesses in permission management, delegate prohibited operations to other agents, or continue operating after its authorization has been revoked. More fundamentally, the components responsible for monitoring and constraining AI agents may themselves become compromised or exercise authority beyond their legitimate mandate. This dissertation addresses the resulting guardian paradox: the enforcement component that prevents dangerous behavior may itself behave dangerously.

The dissertation proposes Human-Critical Security Invariance (HuCSI), a research principle stating that selected protections essential to human agency and system security should not be removable solely through the decisions or self-modification of the AI agents subject to those protections. The proposed implementation architecture, the Kernel of Security Invariance (KSI), separates cognitive agents, policy enforcement, and human governance into distinct trust domains. Cognitive agents may propose actions and contest decisions, but they do not determine their own permissions or modify the mechanisms that enforce those permissions.

The work is purely theoretical. No experiments were conducted, no implementation was constructed, no code was trained, and no proof has been machine-checked. All results are paper proofs derived from stated axioms, and all comparative statements are falsifiable hypotheses rather than empirical findings.

Four formal contributions are developed. First, a semantic attenuation lattice replaces the unsound nominal permission hierarchy of earlier drafts: capabilities carry explicit semantic permission sets, the attenuation order is set inclusion on those sets, and delegation is a monotone non-expanding map on the lattice. Second, a complete inductive preservation argument for privilege safety is constructed, proving that the role bound is preserved by every permitted transition, with the policy-update case handled explicitly through a stated monotonicity or revocation-sweep assumption. Third, an atomic commit predicate is defined such that revocation and execution are linearized in a single authoritative state domain, making revocation soundness provable for in-flight and descendant operations. Fourth, bounded intervention is formalized using a persistent, non-resettable intervention timestamp, establishing that emergency authority cannot be renewed by the component that holds it.

Eight invariants are stated over the transition system: privilege bounds, policy integrity, delegation non-amplification, revocation effectiveness, bounded intervention, non-self-expansion of KSI authority, detection-does-not-confer-authority, and governance separation. The guardian paradox result is deliberately narrowed: the model establishes only that the enforcement component cannot perform unauthorized self-expansion. It does not establish that a guardian cannot cause harm through authorized actions, faulty policy, compromised governance, or excessive but technically authorized intervention.

The dissertation distinguishes three levels of assurance — model-level, implementation-level, and governance-level — and enforces that distinction in every theorem and hypothesis. A model-level paper proof does not imply an implementation guarantee; an implementation guarantee does not imply normative legitimacy. Verification obligations are registered for TLC model checking and TLAPS proof, with explicit acknowledgement of the toolchain's documented limitations and of the refinement gap between specification and implementation.

The expected contribution is a reproducible formal framework that clarifies which security properties can be proved, which depend on implementation assumptions, which require legitimate human governance, and which remain fundamentally unresolved.

Keywords: AI safety, human agency, formal verification, TLA+, security kernel, capability attenuation, privilege escalation, runtime enforcement, multi-agent systems, bounded intervention, corrigibility, human governance, critical invariants.

Chapter 1 — Introduction

1.1 The Problem

Advanced AI systems are increasingly organized as networks of interacting agents that plan, delegate, execute, persist, and cooperate. A model-level safety mechanism attempts to influence what a model produces. A multi-agent security architecture must control what the system can execute, what authority can be delegated, how permissions evolve, and who may revise the normative framework itself.

The central theoretical problem is:

How can a multi-agent AI system enforce critical security and human-control constraints even when its cognitive agents, supervisory components, or delegated workflows behave adversarially or become compromised — without the enforcement component itself becoming an unbounded authority?

Two sub-problems follow. The technical one: can an agent be prevented from acquiring or exercising authority beyond its permitted role? The institutional one: who legitimately defines the protections, resolves conflicts, and authorizes exceptions?

1.2 Three Levels of Assurance

This dissertation distinguishes three levels at which a claim may hold:

Level Established by This dissertation
Model-level Formal transition system and stated assumptions Paper proofs only
Implementation-level Refinement from model under documented hardware/software assumptions Stated, not discharged
Governance-level Legitimate authority and accountability of rule-makers Not formalized

A model-level proof does not imply an implementation-level guarantee. An implementation-level guarantee does not imply governance legitimacy. This distinction is enforced throughout.

1.3 The Guardian Paradox

The guardian paradox: the component responsible for preventing dangerous behavior may itself behave dangerously.

Existing formal work constrains the agents. This dissertation formalizes constraints on the guardian — the enforcement component — through bounded intervention.

1.4 Scope

The dissertation does not claim to solve alignment, guarantee permanent safety, establish moral legitimacy, or reverse irreversible physical effects. Claims are conditional on stated assumptions.

