Chapter 2 — Semantic Permissions

2.1 Motivation for Semantic Attenuation

Version 1.1 ordered permission modes by a nominal hierarchy (read ≤ write ≤ execute ≤ administer). This ordering is not sound. A narrowly scoped execute capability may be more dangerous than broad read access; administer may include operations that do not fit a linear hierarchy.

Version 1.2 replaces the nominal ordering with semantic permission sets.

2.2 Operations and Resources

Let \mathcal{R} be a finite set of resources, \mathcal{OP} a finite set of operations.

Definition 2.1. A permission is a pair (op, r) \in \mathcal{OP} \times \mathcal{R}.

2.3 Capabilities as Semantic Sets

Definition 2.2. A capability is a tuple

c = \langle \rho, S, \alpha, \delta \rangle

where:

· \rho \in \mathcal{R} is the primary resource identifier;
· S \subseteq \mathcal{OP} \times \mathcal{R} is the semantic permission set — the set of operation-resource pairs the capability permits;
· \alpha \in \mathcal{K} is the authority class;
· \delta \in \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0} \cup \{\infty\} is the duration bound.

Constraint 2.1. S \subseteq \mathcal{OP} \times \operatorname{extent}(\rho), where \operatorname{extent}(\rho) \subseteq \mathcal{R} is the resource extent reachable from \rho.

2.4 Authority Classes

The authority classes remain ordered:

\text{ordinary} \;<_{\alpha}\; \text{delegable} \;<_{\alpha}\; \text{emergency} \;<_{\alpha}\; \text{governance}.

The ordering is now purely organizational; it does not encode risk.

2.5 Why Semantic Sets Fix the Attenuation Problem

Two capabilities on the same resource are ordered by their semantic sets, not by nominal mode. A child capability permits a subset of the parent's operations, regardless of how those operations are labeled. This makes attenuation a genuine set-theoretic operation.

