Chapter 3 — Attenuation as Semantic Subset

3.1 The Attenuation Order

Definition 3.1. Define \sqsubseteq on \mathcal{C} by

c_1 \sqsubseteq c_2 \;\Longleftrightarrow\; \rho_1 = \rho_2 \;\land\; S_1 \subseteq S_2 \;\land\; \alpha_1 \leq_{\alpha} \alpha_2 \;\land\; \delta_1 \leq \delta_2.

Proposition 3.1. (\mathcal{C}, \sqsubseteq) is a partial order.

Proof. Reflexivity, antisymmetry, transitivity follow from the corresponding properties of =, \subseteq, \leq_{\alpha}, and \leq on durations. \square

3.2 Downward Closure

Definition 3.2. \operatorname{desc}(c) = \{ c' \in \mathcal{C} : c' \sqsubseteq c \}.

Proposition 3.2. \operatorname{desc} is monotone.

Proof. If c_1 \sqsubseteq c_2 and c' \sqsubseteq c_1, then c' \sqsubseteq c_2. \square

3.3 Meets

Proposition 3.3. For c_1, c_2 with \rho_1 = \rho_2,

c_1 \sqcap c_2 = \langle \rho_1, S_1 \cap S_2, \min_{\alpha}(\alpha_1, \alpha_2), \min(\delta_1, \delta_2) \rangle

is the greatest lower bound.

Proof. The intersection is the greatest subset of both S_1 and S_2. The minima are the greatest lower bounds in the respective orders. \square

3.4 Consequence for Delegation

Delegation is now a subset operation on semantic permission sets. Escalation is inexpressible in the order: no child capability can permit an operation-resource pair the parent did not permit.

