Chapter 7 — Semantic Delegation Theory

7.1 Delegation

Definition 7.1. A delegation (a, b, c_{\text{child}}) requires a parent c_{\text{parent}} \in \Gamma(a) with c_{\text{child}} \sqsubseteq c_{\text{parent}}.

7.2 Non-Amplification

Theorem 7.1 (Delegation non-amplification). Under the delegation transition, every capability held after any finite delegation sequence has semantic permission set contained in that of some initially held capability.

Proof. By induction on length n.

Base (n = 0). Trivial.

Step. Suppose after n delegations, every held c satisfies S(c) \subseteq S(c_0) for some initial c_0. The (n+1)-th delegation produces c_{\text{child}} with c_{\text{child}} \sqsubseteq c_{\text{parent}}. Then S(c_{\text{child}}) \subseteq S(c_{\text{parent}}) \subseteq S(c_0). \square

Corollary 7.1. \operatorname{Auth}(b) \setminus \operatorname{Auth}(a) = \emptyset after any single delegation from a to b.

7.3 Onward Delegation

Definition 7.2. Onward delegation permitted only if \alpha_{\text{parent}} \geq_{\alpha} \text{delegable}.

Proposition 7.1. Onward delegation preserves non-amplification.

Proof. Each step is a semantic subset; induction applies. \square

