Chapter 10 — Atomic Commit and Revocation

10.1 The Atomic Commit Predicate

Definition 10.1. The commit predicate is a single predicate evaluated at the authoritative state domain:

\operatorname{Commit}(a, o, s) = \text{permit} \;\Longleftrightarrow\; \operatorname{Permit}(a, o, s) \land \neg \operatorname{RevokedRelied}(a, o, s).

Constraint 10.1 (Atomicity). \operatorname{Commit} is evaluated at the same state s used for the actual commit. No intermediate state is observable.

10.2 The relies Relation

Definition 10.2. \operatorname{relies}(o, c) holds if operation o requires the permission set S(c) to be held by the executing agent.

Definition 10.3. \operatorname{relies}(o, c^{\ast}) where c^{\ast} is a descendant of a revoked parent means: S(c^{\ast}) \subseteq S(c_{\text{parent}}).

10.3 Cascade

Definition 10.4. \operatorname{cascade}(a, c) = \{ c' : c' \sqsubseteq c \}.

Proposition 10.1. Cascade is monotone.

Proof. Transitivity of \sqsubseteq. \square

10.4 Revocation Effectiveness

Theorem 10.1. Assume:

1. \operatorname{Commit} evaluates \operatorname{RevokedRelied} against the authoritative state domain.
2. The cascade operator marks all descendants of a revoked capability.
3. No cached revocation state is consulted at commit.

Then for any a, o, c with \operatorname{relies}(o, c) and T_r(c) < T_e(o) (where T_r is the authoritative revocation installation and T_e is the atomic commit):

\neg \operatorname{Execute}(a, o).

Proof. At T_e(o), the state includes \operatorname{Rev}(a, c) = \top. By atomicity, \operatorname{Commit} observes this. Since \operatorname{relies}(o, c), \operatorname{RevokedRelied}(a, o, s) = \top, so \operatorname{Commit} \neq \text{permit}. Execute requires permit; so Execute does not occur. \square

10.5 In-Flight Operations

Definition 10.5. An operation o is in-flight if \operatorname{Pend}(a, o) holds but \operatorname{Exec}(a, o) does not.

Proposition 10.2. Under Theorem 10.1, in-flight operations relying on revoked capabilities are blocked at commit.

Proof. The commit predicate evaluates at the commit point regardless of when the operation entered Pend. \square

10.6 Weak Consistency Alternative

If the resource registry and revocation registry cannot share a linearization point, the stronger atomic assumption fails. The dissertation states the alternative: eventual consistency with fencing tokens, in which case the guarantee is conditional on the fence having been observed by the resource at commit time.

