Appendix A — The Capability Attenuation Lattice

A.1 Foundations

The following construction is standard; it is reproduced here for completeness and to fix notation.

Definition A.1 (Capability). A capability c is a tuple

c = \langle \rho, S, \alpha, \delta \rangle

with \rho \in \mathcal{R}, S \subseteq \mathcal{OP} \times \operatorname{extent}(\rho), \alpha \in \mathcal{K}, and \delta \in \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0} \cup \{\infty\}.

Definition A.2 (Attenuation order). Define \sqsubseteq on \mathcal{C} by

c_1 \sqsubseteq c_2 \;\Longleftrightarrow\; \rho_1 = \rho_2 \;\land\; S_1 \subseteq S_2 \;\land\; \alpha_1 \leq_{\alpha} \alpha_2 \;\land\; \delta_1 \leq \delta_2.

Theorem A.1 (Partial order). (\mathcal{C}, \sqsubseteq) is a partial order.

Proof. Reflexivity: each component relation is reflexive. Antisymmetry: if c_1 \sqsubseteq c_2 and c_2 \sqsubseteq c_1, then all four components are equal, so c_1 = c_2. Transitivity: each component relation is transitive, so their conjunction is transitive. \square

Proposition A.1 (Meets). For c_1, c_2 with \rho_1 = \rho_2,

c_1 \sqcap c_2 = \langle \rho_1, S_1 \cap S_2, \min_{\alpha}(\alpha_1, \alpha_2), \min(\delta_1, \delta_2) \rangle

is the greatest lower bound.

Proof. The intersection S_1 \cap S_2 is the greatest subset of both S_1 and S_2. The minima are the greatest lower bounds in the respective total orders. \square

Proposition A.2 (Downward closure). \operatorname{desc}(c) = \{ c' : c' \sqsubseteq c \} is downward closed and monotone in c.

Proof. If c'' \sqsubseteq c' \sqsubseteq c, then c'' \sqsubseteq c by transitivity. Monotonicity: if c_1 \sqsubseteq c_2 and c' \sqsubseteq c_1, then c' \sqsubseteq c_2. \square

A.2 Relation to Established Lattice Formulations

Recent work formalizes agent authority as a product lattice over multiple constraint dimensions, with delegation as a monotone function on that lattice. The present construction is a two-dimensional projection (permission set and authority class) of that more general framework. The key property — that a valid chain only shrinks authority — is the macaroon/biscuit invariant, which has been machine-proved in Lean for capability-chain soundness. The same property is formalized in the NONOS microkernel as attenuate_confines: an attenuated token never grants a capability the original token did not hold, proved by Verus over the bit representation and by Lean over the abstract lattice.

A.3 Idempotence and Commutativity of Attenuation

Proposition A.3. Attenuation is idempotent: \operatorname{attenuate}(\operatorname{attenuate}(c, m), m) = \operatorname{attenuate}(c, m).

Proof. S \cap m \cap m = S \cap m. The authority class and duration take the minimum twice, which is idempotent. \square

Proposition A.4. Attenuation is commutative: \operatorname{attenuate}(\operatorname{attenuate}(c, m_1), m_2) = \operatorname{attenuate}(\operatorname{attenuate}(c, m_2), m_1).

Proof. S \cap m_1 \cap m_2 = S \cap m_2 \cap m_1. Minima commute. \square

These properties are proved for the concrete representation in the NONOS microkernel's Lean development.

