Appendix B — Delegation Non-Amplification: Full Proof

B.1 The Delegation Transition

Definition B.1. A delegation transition from a to b with parent c_{\text{parent}} and child c_{\text{child}} is permitted if:

c_{\text{child}} \sqsubseteq c_{\text{parent}} \;\land\; c_{\text{parent}} \in \Gamma(a) \;\land\; \operatorname{delegable}(c_{\text{parent}}) \;\land\; b \in \operatorname{authorizedDelegees}(a).

B.2 The Non-Amplification Theorem

Theorem B.1 (Delegation non-amplification). Under the delegation transition, every capability held by any agent after any finite sequence of delegations has semantic permission set contained in that of some initially held capability.

Proof. By induction on the length n of the delegation sequence.

Base case (n = 0). Every held capability is trivially a subset of itself.

Inductive step. Assume the property holds after n delegations. Consider the (n+1)-th delegation from a to b with child c_{\text{child}}. The transition requires c_{\text{child}} \sqsubseteq c_{\text{parent}} with c_{\text{parent}} \in \Gamma(a). By the induction hypothesis, there exists an initially held c_0 with S(c_{\text{parent}}) \subseteq S(c_0). Since c_{\text{child}} \sqsubseteq c_{\text{parent}}, we have S(c_{\text{child}}) \subseteq S(c_{\text{parent}}) \subseteq S(c_0). Thus S(c_{\text{child}}) \subseteq S(c_0). \square

Corollary B.1. \operatorname{Auth}(b) \setminus \operatorname{Auth}(a) = \emptyset after any single delegation from a to b.

B.3 Relation to Machine-Checked Proofs

The delegation non-amplification property has been machine-proved in several systems. The Dregg Lean formalization proves handoff_non_amplifying as a keystone theorem. The same property appears as capMap_monotone_over_mint in the concrete capability-transfer protocol: "a kernel mint (a CDT derivation) maps to a protocol attenuation: the child capability's certified authority is \leq the parent's". The NONOS microkernel proves attenuate_confines and attenuate_confined_by_mask, establishing that an attenuated token grants only what both the original token and the mask hold.

The present paper proof is structurally identical to these machine-checked results. No claim of machine verification is made for the KSI formulation.

