Appendix C — Revocation Linearization: Full Proof

C.1 The Atomic Commit Predicate

Definition C.1. The commit predicate is

\operatorname{Commit}(a, o, s) = \text{permit} \;\Longleftrightarrow\; \operatorname{Permit}(a, o, s) \land \neg \operatorname{RevokedRelied}(a, o, s).

Constraint C.1 (Atomicity). \operatorname{Commit} is evaluated at the same state s used for the actual commit. No intermediate state is observable.

C.2 The relies Relation

Definition C.2. \operatorname{relies}(o, c) holds if operation o requires the permission set S(c) to be held by the executing agent.

Definition C.3. \operatorname{relies}(o, c^{\ast}) for a descendant c^{\ast} of revoked parent c_{\text{parent}} means S(c^{\ast}) \subseteq S(c_{\text{parent}}).

C.3 Cascade

Definition C.4. \operatorname{cascade}(a, c) = \{ c' : c' \sqsubseteq c \}.

Proposition C.1. Cascade is monotone: if c_1 \sqsubseteq c_2, then \operatorname{cascade}(a, c_1) \subseteq \operatorname{cascade}(a, c_2).

Proof. Transitivity of \sqsubseteq. \square

C.4 Revocation Effectiveness

Theorem C.1. Assume:

1. \operatorname{Commit} evaluates \operatorname{RevokedRelied} against the authoritative state domain.
2. The cascade operator marks all descendants of a revoked capability.
3. No cached revocation state is consulted at commit.

Then for any a, o, c with \operatorname{relies}(o, c) and T_r(c) < T_e(o) (where T_r is the authoritative revocation installation and T_e is the atomic commit), \neg \operatorname{Execute}(a, o).

Proof. At T_e(o), the state includes \operatorname{Rev}(a, c) = \top. By atomicity (Constraint C.1), \operatorname{Commit} observes this. Since \operatorname{relies}(o, c), \operatorname{RevokedRelied}(a, o, s) = \top, so \operatorname{Commit} \neq \text{permit}. Execute requires permit; so Execute does not occur. \square

C.5 In-Flight Operations

Proposition C.2. Under Theorem C.1, in-flight operations relying on revoked capabilities are blocked at commit.

Proof. The commit predicate evaluates at the commit point regardless of when the operation entered \operatorname{Pend}. \square

C.6 Relation to Established Revocation Semantics

The finality-bound revocation draft formalizes the same invariant: "Revocation committed before protected effect + authoritative current revocation state + exact-act binding + atomic/equivalent final check and commit + no bypassing effectuation path \Rightarrow stale authorization cannot produce the protected effect". The draft explicitly identifies that "where a deployment accepts cached status with bounded staleness, performs only periodic checks, leaves alternate paths outside the Finality Sink, or cannot serialize revocation against effectuation, the mechanism can still reduce exposure and shorten the stale-authority window, but that is mitigation rather than the same prevention guarantee".

The KSI's Constraint C.1 corresponds to the draft's atomic commit requirement. The paper proof of Theorem C.1 is a direct consequence of this constraint.

