Appendix D — Bounded Intervention: Full Proof

D.1 The Persistent Timestamp

Constraint D.1 (Non-resettable entry). When \operatorname{Int} transitions from Normal to any non-Normal state, t_0 is set to t. No KSI action modifies t_0 except to reset it to \bot when \operatorname{Int} returns to Normal.

Constraint D.2 (No reset without return). t_0 cannot be updated while \operatorname{Int} \neq \text{Normal}.

D.2 The Bounded Intervention Invariant

Invariant I-5.

\Box \; \left( \operatorname{Int} \neq \text{Normal} \Longrightarrow t - t_0 \leq T_{\max} \right).

Proof obligation by case analysis.

Init. t_0 = \bot, so the antecedent \operatorname{Int} \neq \text{Normal} is false. Trivial.

Intervene. Sets t_0 = t. Then t - t_0 = 0 \leq T_{\max}.

Tick. Increases t. Preservation requires that if t - t_0 = T_{\max}, no further Tick is permitted while \operatorname{Int} \neq \text{Normal}. This is a constraint on the Tick action: Tick is blocked when the deadline would be exceeded.

Recover. Returns to Normal, setting t_0 = \bot. Trivial.

Other actions. Do not modify t or t_0. Preservation trivial. \square

D.3 The Deadline Expiration Problem

When t - t_0 = T_{\max} and the threat remains unresolved, safety and automatic expiration conflict. The fallback is a policy parameter:

Constraint D.3 (Fallback policy). At deadline, one of:

· (a) transition to SafeHalt and require human authorization for any further action;
· (b) block all non-Normal operations and require independent authorization for continued intervention;
· (c) permit a single bounded extension only via an independent authorization event, with the extension recorded and bounded.

Invariant I-5 holds regardless of which fallback is chosen, provided the fallback does not reset t_0 without independent authorization.

D.4 Relation to Established Bounded Temporal Formalisms

The reduction of bounded invariance to ordinary invariance via a clock variable is standard in the temporal verification literature. For the bounded-operator style, proof rules exist for establishing bounded-invariance and bounded-response properties of timed transition systems. For the explicit-clock style, "every time-bounded property is a safety property" and standard temporal proof rules apply.

The KSI formulation uses the explicit-clock style. The persistent, non-resettable timestamp is the KSI's specific contribution: without it, repeated interventions could circumvent the bound by resetting the clock.

