Appendix E — The Guardian Paradox: Formal Statement and Narrowed Resolution

E.1 Formal Statement

Definition E.1. A guardian is a component g \in A that enforces invariants on other agents.

Definition E.2. An agent a self-expands if a transition modifies \operatorname{Auth}(a) without an independent authorization event.

Definition E.3. The guardian paradox is the situation in which g self-expands during the enforcement of an invariant.

E.2 The Narrowed Theorem

Theorem E.1 (Narrowed guardian paradox result). Under I-6, a KSI agent cannot perform unauthorized self-expansion within the model. That is: no transition modifies \operatorname{Auth}(a) for a \in A_{\text{KSI}} without an independent authorization event.

Proof. I-6 states \Box \; \forall a \in A_{\text{KSI}} : \operatorname{Auth}(a) \subseteq \operatorname{Auth}_{\text{granted}}(a). The write sets of all KSI actions exclude the KSI authority-grant mechanism. Any modification to \operatorname{Auth}(a) for a \in A_{\text{KSI}} must therefore come from an action outside the KSI's own transition set — that is, from an independent authorization event. \square

E.3 Non-Theorem

Non-theorem E.1. The model does not establish that a KSI agent cannot cause harm through authorized actions. A guardian could still cause harm through an initially authorized capability, a faulty policy, a compromised governance principal, or an intervention that is technically authorized but destructive.

Prevention of harmful authorized action requires additional policy constraints and is outside the scope of I-6. This limitation is stated explicitly rather than concealed.

E.4 Relation to Established Discussions

The guardian paradox is related to the containment paradox in AI safety: the conditions under which amplification preserves alignment involve alignment-preservation, property-fidelity, and absence of deceptive mesa-optimisation in the amplifying components. The structural problem is that "even granting amplification within those conditions, the amplified process itself must be trusted or audited at a capacity level exceeding the human-side verifier, and the structural problem of who or what supplies that trust does not disappear — it reappears one level up".

The KSI's bounded intervention formalization addresses a specific, tractable instance of this general problem: preventing the enforcement component from expanding its own authority, rather than preventing an arbitrary superintelligence from doing so.

