Appendix F — Trusted Computing Base and Layer Guarantees

F.1 Formal TCB Definition

Definition F.1. The trusted computing base T of a system S is the minimal set of components whose correct behavior is necessary for S's security properties to hold.

F.2 Layer Guarantees

Layer Component Guarantee Fails if
L_0 Hardware / OS Memory isolation Hardware compromise
L_1 KSI enforcement Authorization at execution L_1 state modified by L_4
L_2 Policy decision point Policy evaluation consistent with \pi L_2 compromised or \pi tampered
L_3 Governance Legitimate authorization Credentials compromised
L_5 Audit Tamper-evident record L_1 and L_5 jointly compromised

F.3 Relation to Established Kernel Verification

The seL4 microkernel is the first operating-system kernel with a machine-checked proof of implementation correctness, subject to assumptions about the compiler, assembly, and hardware. The seL4 verification comprises over 200,000 lines of Isabelle/HOL and about 8,700 lines of C. The verification covers functional correctness, integrity, and confidentiality, but not all security properties.

The KSI's TCB is larger than seL4's, because it includes the policy decision point and governance layer. The KSI does not claim seL4-level assurance for any component. The TCB is documented explicitly so that the assumptions are visible.

F.4 Reference Monitor Requirements

The canonical reference monitor requirements, dating to Anderson (1972), are: complete mediation, tamperproofness, and verifiability. The KSI enforcement boundary satisfies complete mediation by construction (Chapter 20). Tamperproofness is conditional on L_0 and L_1 integrity. Verifiability is aspirational: the TLA+ specification is intended to be small enough for analysis, but no machine-checked proof has been constructed.

